From 5a1e0c7d2b9f4e6a8c3d1f0b7e9a2c4d6f8b0a1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shiro <build@shiro>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Shiro: mmap(0) checks the whole range it returns (FindVirtual
 counted a missing page table as free from its start)

FindVirtual walks the page tables from virt and, where an entry at some level
is missing, counts that entry's whole span (2 MiB, 1 GiB, 512 GiB) as free.
But virt + got is in the middle of the span unless it happens to be aligned,
so every such step counted more than was looked at, and the last
(virt & (span - 1)) bytes of the range were never checked. A mapping there
was handed out a second time.

HotSpot met it (tabcomputer#122): glibc's arena for the JVM's main thread is
mmap(0, 128 MiB), trimmed to a 64 MiB boundary, which leaves a hole of just
under 64 MiB below it at an unaligned address. With -Xint or
-XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1 the next 64 MiB reservation (the class space's
aligned reserve) was placed in that hole, over the arena's first 0x4c000
bytes, and released again: the thread's tcache and Thread object were gone,
malloc faulted, and the JVM's signal handler faulted on the Thread until the
stack ran out (exit 139).

A missing entry now counts only to the end of its span.
---
 blink/memorymalloc.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/blink/memorymalloc.c b/blink/memorymalloc.c
index 0000000..0000000 100644
--- a/blink/memorymalloc.c
+++ b/blink/memorymalloc.c
@@ -1056,7 +1056,7 @@ StartOver:
                    (((virt + got) >> i) & 511) * 8);
       if (i == 12 || !(pt & PAGE_V)) break;
     }
-    got += (u64)1 << i;
+    got += ((u64)1 << i) - ((virt + got) & (((u64)1 << i) - 1));
     if ((pt & PAGE_V)) {
       virt += got;
       goto StartOver;
